  <section>
    <h2 id="s1"><span class="secnum">1</span>What goes in, what comes out</h2>
    <aside class="note"><b>The residual</b> is the median distance, in pixels of the stack, between each detected star
    and the Gaia star the solution puts there. The block reports it with the number of stars it was measured on.</aside>
    <p>The block reads the stack and finds its stars (a background mesh of 64 pixels, a threshold of 15 times the noise,
    6 pixels at least). It also reads what the project knows of the field: the target, the telescope pointing written
    in the files, the focal length and the pixel size.</p>
    <p>It writes the world coordinate system of the stack: a tangent-plane projection with a distortion polynomial
    (the SIP convention). It also reports the centre of the field, its size, the scale in arcseconds per pixel, the
    angle of north, whether the image is mirrored, the residual of the fit and, when the pixel size is known, the focal
    length the solution implies. The Report also gives the residual in arcseconds.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s2"><span class="secnum">2</span>The first solution</h2>
    <p><strong>Asterion first.</strong> The first solution comes from Asterion,<a class="cite" href="#ref-1">[1]</a>
    shipped with the installer. It is given at most 10 seconds. When the target’s position is known, it is searched near
    it; otherwise on the whole sky, if Blind solving is on. When the scale comes from the focal length and Asterion finds
    nothing at that scale, it is asked again at any scale, in case the focal length was entered wrongly. The fine fit of
    section 3 starts from this solution, and the search below is skipped.</p>
    <p><strong>FLOAT’s own search.</strong> When Asterion is not installed, cannot run or finds nothing, FLOAT’s own
    search finds the field. The log says which case it was. The search runs in four steps.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The scale.</strong> It is 206.265 × pixel size (µm) / focal length (mm), in arcseconds per pixel, divided by
      the drizzle scale. When it
      is unknown, 22 values from 0.4 to 40 arcseconds per pixel are tried, the focal lengths this camera was solved at
      before first.</li>
      <li><strong>The match.</strong> Triangle matching of the 150 brightest detections with the 150 brightest Gaia
      stars projected at the trial scale, both mirror images tried. Fifteen matched pairs are taken as a match.</li>
      <li><strong>The centre.</strong> The given centre first, then centres shifted by the Search range, a fraction of
      the field.</li>
      <li><strong>The catalogue depth.</strong> The faintest Gaia magnitude is set by the field’s area, from G 10.5 for
      a wide field to G 16 for a small one.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The random draws of the match are seeded: the same stack gives the same solution at every run.</p>

    <h3>Blind solving</h3>
    <p>When the centre is unknown, or the guided search fails, the field is identified on the whole sky from its
    brightest stars (up to 80). The principle is the one of Astrometry.net:<a class="cite" href="#ref-2">[2]</a> each
    group of four stars is coded by the positions of its two inner stars in the frame where its two most distant stars
    stand at (0, 0) and (1, 1), a code that does not change with translation, rotation or scale. The codes of the image
    are looked up in an index of the sky built from the bright stars of the Hipparcos and Tycho-2
    catalogues.<a class="cite" href="#ref-3">[3]</a> Each candidate is verified by counting how many other stars of the
    image fall on catalogue stars, and is kept only when that count stands well above what chance gives. The index
    holds the stars of Hipparcos to V 9.5 and of Tycho-2 to VT 11, so the blind search is made for fields wider than
    about 1 degree.</p>

    <h3>Fields of few stars</h3>
    <p>Under 50 stars in the stack, the block uses another decision. Every triple of the 30 brightest stars is compared
    with every triple of the catalogue stars. A solution stands only when its Bayes factor reaches 10<sup>9</sup>, by the
    decision of Lang et al.:<a class="cite" href="#ref-2">[2]</a> each star either has no counterpart (a quarter of them
    are allowed none) or lies within a Gaussian of 1 pixel around a catalogue star. No random draw is used. With fewer
    than 6 stars, no field is identified. No distortion polynomial is fitted, as it would follow the noise of so few
    stars.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s3"><span class="secnum">3</span>The fine fit on Gaia DR3</h2>
    <aside class="note"><b>Epoch.</b> The Gaia positions are at epoch 2016.0. The fit uses them as they are; the
    Catalogs step (section 4 of this page) carries them to the date of the images.</aside>
    <p>The first solution gives the stars’ approximate place on the sky. The fine fit then runs in passes. A
    tangent-plane projection with a distortion polynomial of the chosen order is fitted on up to 4000 detections. The
    Gaia stars are projected through it and matched to the detections within 3 pixels, then the fit is made again on up
    to 8000 detections, and twice more with matches within 1.5 and 1 pixel. Stars within 5 pixels of the edge are not
    used. The median residual of the last pass is the one reported.</p>
    <p>The solution is written once the fit is done. The solution of a sparse field (section 2) must also give a median
    residual under 1 pixel, or no solution is given rather than a wrong one.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s4"><span class="secnum">4</span>What the solution is used for</h2>
    <p>Every later step that uses the sky relies on this solution.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The Gaia catalogues.</strong> The Catalogs step places the Gaia stars of the field on the image,
      their positions carried to the date of the frames with their proper motions, and selects the stars whose spectra
      the <a href="/blocks/color/">Color</a> block uses.</li>
      <li><strong>The H-alpha reference.</strong> A survey of the sky in H-alpha is reprojected onto the stack. It shows
      where the faint nebulosity is, so that the <a href="/blocks/gradient/">Gradient</a> block takes no sky sample there.</li>
      <li><strong>Colour.</strong> The Color block measures the stars the solution places and compares them with
      their Gaia spectra.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>When no field is found.</strong> The setting If the field is not identified decides. With Continue
    without solution, the run goes on, the block is drawn in orange and its tooltip gives the reason. Color then takes
    its fallback balance, and Gradient chooses its samples without the survey. With Stop, the block fails.</p>
    <p>A target far from the pointing recorded in the files is handled in two tries. When the two differ by more than the
    field’s diagonal plus 2 degrees, the typed target is tried first, without the blind solver. If that fails, the
    pointing of the files is tried, and the log asks to check the project’s target.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s5"><span class="secnum">5</span>The offline star catalogue</h2>
    <p>The stars FLOAT reads come from an offline Gaia DR3 catalogue installed once, by the installer or from Settings ›
    Catalogs &amp; astrometry. It derives from published extracts of Gaia DR3.<a class="cite" href="#ref-4">[4]</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-5">[5]</a> The star index of the blind solver is installed in the same place.</p>
    <p>A run downloads nothing. When a catalogue is missing, the block says so and names the setting that installs it.
    A target name is looked up in the names already resolved and in the Messier, NGC, IC, Sharpless and Lynds
    catalogues that FLOAT ships. An unknown name needs its RA and Dec.</p>
    <p>The extract keeps the 127 brightest stars of every cell of 14 arcminutes. In the Milky Way it can stop short of
    the magnitude asked for; it is enough for a solution. Without the extract, the block reads the sky tiles of the cache, and
    fails with the reason when they are not all there.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s6"><span class="secnum">6</span>Settings</h2>
    <div class="tbl-wrap wide">
      <table class="tbl">
        <caption><b>Table 1.</b>The settings of the block. The Advanced ones are marked.</caption>
        <thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Value</th><th>Range</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Target name</th><td>from the project or the files</td><td>text</td><td>Name of the main object, resolved to the approximate centre of the field. Not needed when RA and Dec are given or when the files hold the pointing.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Centre RA</th><td>from the project or the files</td><td class="n">0 – 360 °</td><td>Approximate right ascension of the centre. Within about 20 % of the field width is enough.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Centre Dec</th><td>from the project or the files</td><td class="n">−90 – 90 °</td><td>Approximate declination of the centre.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Focal length</th><td>from the files or the project</td><td class="n">mm, above 0</td><td>With the pixel size, it gives the image scale. Empty, the scale is searched (a few minutes without Asterion).</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Pixel size (Advanced)</th><td>from the camera or the files</td><td class="n">µm, above 0</td><td>Size of a sensor pixel.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Image scale (Advanced)</th><td>computed, else searched</td><td class="n">arcsec/px, above 0</td><td>Size of a pixel of the stack on the sky.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Blind solving (Advanced)</th><td>on</td><td>on, off</td><td>Identifies the field on the whole sky when the centre is unknown or the guided search fails.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Search range (Advanced)</th><td class="n">0.2 × field</td><td class="n">0 – 1, step 0.05</td><td>How far from the given centre the search looks for the field.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Distortion order (Advanced)</th><td class="n">3</td><td class="n">0 – 5</td><td>Order of the SIP polynomial: 3 for refractors with reducers and wide fields, 2 for small fields, 0 for a pure tangent plane.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">If the field is not identified</th><td>Continue without solution</td><td>Continue without solution, Stop</td><td>What the block does when no field is identified (section 4).</td></tr>
